⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
It can act as:

TCP Client
TCP Server (Listener)
UDP Client
UDP Server
Port Scanner
File Transfer Tool
Service Testing Tool
Network Debugging Tool

Think of it as a tool that allows you to create raw TCP/UDP connections without needing a real application.


 ✅ 1. Client And Listener
TCP requires a listener on one side and a client initiating the connection on the other. On macOS and many OpenBSD-based implementations:
# Terminal A: listen on TCP 9000
nc -l 9000
# Terminal B: connect to that listener
nc 127.0.0.1 9000

Type into either terminal and press Enter; the other terminal receives it. The connection is bidirectional. Ctrl-C terminates it; EOF handling differs between implementations. Check nc -h: traditional Netcat may use nc -l -p 9000, while Nmap’s Ncat uses ncat -l 9000. Do not assume flags are portable.

 ✅ 2. Send Messages
With the listener running, send a single message:
printf 'hello from the client\n' | nc -N 127.0.0.1 9000


-N shuts down the socket’s write direction after stdin reaches EOF on implementations supporting it. Some Linux variants instead use -q 1. Without suitable EOF handling, the command may remain open after sending everything. Prefer printf to echo when exact bytes and line endings matter. TCP transports a byte stream, not discrete messages; the receiver needs delimiters, lengths, or another framing convention.

 ✅ 3. Transfer A File
Run the receiver first, then the sender. These examples use OpenBSD-style nc:
# Receiver: this overwrites received.bin if it already exists
nc -l 9000 > received.bin
# Sender
nc -N <RECEIVER_IP> 9000 < source.bin
# Compare hashes on the respective machines
shasum -a 256 source.bin
shasum -a 256 received.bin

Matching hashes establish byte-for-byte integrity, not sender identity. Plain nc provides no application-level authentication or encryption; use SSH/SCP for real transfers over untrusted networks. A successful sender exit alone does not establish that the receiver saved the complete file.

 ✅ 4. Transfer A Directory With tar
Use a controlled destination and a trusted sender:
# Receiver
mkdir -p /tmp/nc-received
nc -l 9000 | tar -xf - -C /tmp/nc-received
# Sender: send lab-data relative to its parent directory
tar -cf - -C /path/to/parent lab-data | nc -N <RECEIVER_IP> 9000


tar -cf - -C /path/to/parent lab-data | nc -N <RECEIVER_IP> 9000
